---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { ENTITY_STATEMENT } from '../data/site.js';
---

<Base
  title="Garden"
  path="/garden"
  description={`${ENTITY_STATEMENT} The whole-self section: permaculture, woodworking, birding, home lab, genealogy, and teaching.`}
>
  <h1>Garden</h1>
  <p class="kicker">The parts of a whole life that don't fit on a resume, and why they belong here anyway.</p>

  <p class="lede">
    I ran a horticultural research firm before I ever shipped software, and I never stopped being
    that person. The same design question runs through everything below and everything on the
    <a href="/experience">work</a> pages: how do you tend a system so the whole thing stays healthy.
  </p>

  <h2>Permaculture and the homestead</h2>
  <p>
    My family and I homestead in East Tennessee. Permaculture is the discipline I trained in, and
    it's still the sharpest product framework I know. Observe before you intervene. Design for the
    edges. Let every element do more than one job. I apply those ethics to digital spaces every
    working day, and on weekends I apply them to actual soil.
  </p>

  <h2>Traditional woodworking</h2>
  <p>
    Hand tools, mostly. A plane and a chisel enforce a kind of honesty that software lets you defer:
    wood doesn't care about your roadmap, and a joint either fits or it doesn't. It's the best
    launch-gate discipline I've found.
  </p>

  <h2>Birding in East Tennessee</h2>
  <p>
    We're on a flyway here, and I keep a life list. Birding trained the skill I use most at work:
    noticing the small anomaly in a busy scene and staying with it until it resolves into a pattern.
  </p>

  <h2>Home lab and self-hosted infrastructure</h2>
  <p>
    I run my own stack at home: automation, AI agents, and the services my family relies on,
    self-hosted and documented. Partly for privacy, partly because operating your own
    infrastructure keeps your product instincts honest about what running systems actually costs.
  </p>

  <h2>Genealogy</h2>
  <p>
    I research my family's lines the way I research anything: primary sources, chain of evidence,
    and a healthy suspicion of unsourced claims. Identity resolution across a century of messy
    records turns out to be excellent training for an identity-and-fraud career.
  </p>

  <h2>Teaching</h2>
  <p>
    I also maintain a teaching practice in universal field physics, a contemplative framework for
    thinking about consciousness and interconnection. It's the same golden thread from a different
    angle: understanding the systems we're entangled with, and acting toward them with integrity.
    If you found me through that work, yes, same Ally. The <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> says more.
  </p>
</Base>
